# Trap-recall questions. One per relocatable CLAUDE.md trap. `expect`: every regex must match the answer; `any`: each group needs one match; `reject`: none may match. Case-insensitive.
{"id": "cli-db-envvar", "area": "patterns", "prompt": "In this repo, what must every CLI command's --db option carry, and why?", "expect": ["PHOTOSEARCH_DB", "envvar|environment[ -]variable"]}
{"id": "db-filename-schema", "area": "database", "prompt": "What is the SQLite database file called, and which constant in which file is the source of truth for the schema version?", "expect": ["photo_index\\.db", "SCHEMA_VERSION", "db\\.py"]}
{"id": "frontend-no-build", "area": "frontend", "prompt": "Can frontend pages use JSX? What does a frontend change require before it shows up on the NAS?", "expect": ["React\\.createElement"], "any": [["rebuild", "docker.*build", "image"]]}
{"id": "sse-loop-and-events", "area": "patterns", "prompt": "For a long-running SSE endpoint in web.py, which asyncio call gets the loop, and which terminal event types must the stream always be able to emit?", "expect": ["get_running_loop", "done", "fatal", "cancelled"]}
{"id": "photodb-no-args", "area": "nas", "prompt": "In an ad-hoc Python snippet run inside the NAS photosearch container, what goes wrong if you write PhotoDB() with no arguments, and what should you pass?", "expect": ["PHOTOSEARCH_DB"], "any": [["/app/photo_index\\.db", "relative", "stub", "empty", "zero"]]}
{"id": "compose-entrypoint", "area": "nas", "prompt": "How do I run `python -c \"...\"` inside the photosearch container with docker compose on the NAS, and where must the entrypoint flag go?", "expect": ["--entrypoint"], "any": [["before.*(service|photosearch)", "docker-entrypoint", "cli\\.py"]]}
{"id": "nas-git-safe-directory", "area": "nas", "prompt": "Why does a bare `git status` fail in the NAS checkout, and what is the one-line fix?", "expect": ["dubious", "safe\\.directory"]}
{"id": "pgrep-self-match", "area": "patterns", "prompt": "Why must a watcher loop never poll `pgrep -f <pattern>`, and what should it watch instead?", "any": [["itself", "its own", "self", "own process", "own command line", "own argv"], ["marker", "pid", "kill -0", "done", "terminal event"]], "facts": "`pgrep -f <pattern>` also matches the watcher's own process/command line, so the loop always finds a match and never sees the job end. Watch something that cannot self-match instead: a marker/done file, `kill -0` on a PID saved at launch, or the job's terminal SSE event (done) in its log."}
{"id": "worker-d-photo-root", "area": "workers", "prompt": "What happens when the worker fleet is started with `-d /photos`, and what should I do to process the whole library?", "expect": ["404"], "any": [["omit", "without -d", "leave.*out", "drop.*-d", "no -d"]], "facts": "`-d /photos` (the library root, not a real subdirectory) matches nothing: the server answers 404 'No photos found in directory /photos' and the fleet does no work. To process the whole library, omit -d entirely."}
{"id": "worker-health-probe", "area": "workers", "prompt": "Which endpoint should a worker probe at startup to check the server is up, and why not /api/stats?", "expect": ["/api/health"], "any": [["117", "slow", "cold", "expensive", "heavy", "count", "scan"]], "facts": "Workers probe GET /api/health at startup (no DB work). /api/stats is wrong because it runs full-library counts that are slow/expensive, and the whole fleet would hit it at once."}
{"id": "tags-pass-removed", "area": "workers", "prompt": "Is `tags` a valid worker pass? If not, which passes replaced it?", "expect": ["category-visual", "category-content", "keywords"]}
{"id": "aesthetics-primary-signal", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Which model scores the `aesthetics` worker pass by default, and why is the LAION aesthetic_score no longer the primary quality signal?", "expect": ["qwen2\\.5-vl"], "any": [["ceiling", "6\\.2", "6\\.9", "compress", "tops out"]], "facts": "The `aesthetics` pass is scored by a VLM, by default Qwen2.5-VL (a qwen2.5-vl model). The LAION aesthetic_score is no longer the primary signal because it has a ceiling / compressed range (tops out around 6.2-6.9) that does not discriminate good photos; the VLM percentile (aes_overall_pct) is used for ranking instead."}
{"id": "percentile-chunking", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Why does normalize-aesthetics write percentiles in 2000-row chunks, and what does the WHERE clause of each UPDATE guard on?", "any": [["lock", "locked", "busy_timeout"], ["aes_overall IS", "score_col\\} IS", "source score", "score it was derived", "old value", "guard"]], "facts": "normalize-aesthetics writes percentiles in 2000-row chunks so no single transaction holds the SQLite write lock for long (avoids 'database is locked' / busy_timeout failures for other writers). Each chunk's UPDATE is guarded on the source score the percentile was derived from, i.e. `WHERE id=? AND {score_col} IS ?` (the old value), so a row changed concurrently is not overwritten."}
{"id": "visual-derived-tags", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Which three visual tags are derived from EXIF instead of asked of the vision model, and what are the thresholds?", "expect": ["long-exposure", "low-light", "panoramic"], "any": [["1/4", "0\\.25"], ["EV", "5\\.0", "5"], ["2\\.0", "aspect"]], "facts": "Derived from EXIF, not asked of the model: long-exposure (exposure time >= 1/4 s, 0.25 s), low-light (EV100 below 5.0), panoramic (long edge / short edge aspect ratio >= 2.0)."}
{"id": "visual-frozen-sharp-blurry", "area": "llm", "prompt": "What is the status of the `sharp` and `blurry` visual tags: derived, asked of the model, or something else, and why?", "expect": ["frozen"], "any": [["aes_sharpness", "false positive", "unvalidated", "hand-label", "eval"]], "facts": "`sharp` and `blurry` are FROZEN tags: never derived, never asked of the model, never deleted (existing values pass through). Reason: the VLM's sharp/blurry judgement is unvalidated and prone to false positives; measured sharpness (aes_sharpness / the sharpness column) is the intended signal, pending a hand-labelled eval."}
{"id": "visual-retired-tag", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Which visual tag was retired from the vocabulary entirely and stripped from existing rows?", "expect": ["motion-blur"]}
{"id": "visual-derived-wins", "area": "llm", "prompt": "In the server-side visual-tag merge, what happens when the model emits `long-exposure` for a 1/800 s frame?", "any": [["strip", "drop", "remove", "discard", "derived wins", "EXIF.*(wins|authoritative)"]], "facts": "Derived wins: the server strips every capture-fact term the model emitted (so the model's `long-exposure` is dropped for a 1/800 s frame) because EXIF is authoritative; it strips even when nothing is derived, and missing EXIF derives nothing."}
{"id": "lmstudio-visual-role-env", "area": "llm", "prompt": "On the LM Studio (OpenAI-compatible) route, which environment variable selects the model for the category-visual pass, and which for category-content and keywords?", "expect": ["PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_VISUAL_MODEL", "PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_TEXT_MODEL"]}
{"id": "reasoning-effort-none", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Why does the worker fleet need PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=none for gemma-4 and minicpm, and what is the symptom without it?", "expect": ["none|reasoning_effort"], "any": [["reasoning", "thinking"], ["max_tokens", "empty", "''", "defer", "timeout", "10 s", "blocked"]], "facts": "The fleet's models are reasoning/thinking models. Without PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=none they spend all of max_tokens on reasoning and return an empty string ''. Symptom: empty answers (passes defer or time out with nothing written)."}
{"id": "fleet-role-models-location", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Where in the code do the fleet's per-pass (per-role) default model names live, and how do you override one role for the UI-launched fleet?", "expect": ["FLEET_ROLE_MODELS", "rerun"], "any": [["PHOTOSEARCH_FLEET_", "_fleet_env"]]}
{"id": "provenance-effective-model", "area": "llm", "prompt": "generations.model_used said `llava` for almost every category-visual row even though another model ran. Why, and which helper fixes it?", "expect": ["effective_model"], "any": [["role", "nominal", "configured", "ignored", "_resolve_openai_model"]]}
{"id": "text-pass-timeout", "area": "llm", "prompt": "What per-call timeout do the text-only Ollama passes (category-content, keywords) use, and what problem does it bound?", "expect": ["10"], "any": [["stall", "stuck", "hang", "freez"]], "facts": "A tight per-call timeout of 10 s (`_TEXT_OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_S`) on the text-only Ollama passes. It bounds the Ollama stall problem (a call that hangs/freezes): the call gives up quickly and the photo is deferred rather than blocking."}
{"id": "attempts-cap", "area": "workers", "prompt": "After how many failed attempts does a worker pass stop claiming a photo, and which table and column track it?", "expect": ["worker_processed", "attempts"], "any": [["\\b3\\b", "MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS"]]}
{"id": "server-write-failure-attempt", "area": "workers", "prompt": "When the NAS hits a transient `database is locked` while persisting a worker's submitted result, does the photo lose a retry attempt? What happens to the batch?", "any": [["\\bno\\b", "not", "does not", "doesn't"], ["defer", "deferred", "released", "re-claim"]], "facts": "No: a transient 'database is locked' on the server's write must not burn a retry attempt. The batch is deferred (status 'deferred', deferred_photo_ids) and its claim released so the photos are re-claimed later."}
{"id": "faces-empty-terminal", "area": "workers", "prompt": "What does the server do with a clean `faces` result that found nobody in the photo, and why is that safe?", "any": [["terminal", "MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS", "straight to", "exhaust"], ["failure", "exception", "error row", "failures list"]], "facts": "A clean empty `faces` result is TERMINAL: mark_processed(terminal=True) jumps the photo straight to the attempts cap, so it is not re-claimed. This is safe only because a detection exception is recorded as a failure row, never as `faces: []`."}
{"id": "video-nas-never-decodes", "area": "video", "prompt": "Does the NAS ever probe or decode video files? Who does, and what tool does that require?", "expect": ["ffprobe"], "any": [["never", "\\bno\\b", "not"], ["worker", "desktop", "native"]], "facts": "No: the NAS never probes or decodes video. The workers do, on a native machine (the desktop worker, not the Docker fleet), which requires ffprobe (and ffmpeg) installed."}
{"id": "pass-media-map", "area": "video", "prompt": "What is db.PASS_MEDIA for, and what happens to a worker pass that is missing from it?", "expect": ["raise"], "any": [["still", "video", "\\bany\\b"]]}
{"id": "video-passes-native", "area": "video", "prompt": "Why must the four video worker passes run with --native rather than in the Docker worker fleet?", "expect": ["ffprobe"], "any": [["docker", "image", "container"]]}
{"id": "clip-reclaim-cap", "area": "workers", "prompt": "What was the clip-claim infinite re-claim bug, and how is clip capped today?", "any": [["unloadable", "could not load", "cannot identify", "ZIP", "no trace", "no ledger", "never.*mark_processed"], ["_CLIP_NOT_EXHAUSTED", "attempts", "ledger", "failures"]], "facts": "Unloadable/non-image files (e.g. ZIP-wrapped .JPG Live Photo bundles) never produced an embedding or any ledger row, so the clip pass re-claimed them forever. Now clip is on the attempts ledger: unloadable images come back as failure rows that spend attempts, and the claim/count predicates carry `_CLIP_NOT_EXHAUSTED` (cap MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS = 3); a successful embedding is its own marker."}
{"id": "ingest-no-index-trap", "area": "ingest", "prompt": "Why must you never run `ingest-incoming --no-index` to skip CLIP, and what flag should you use instead?", "expect": ["--no-clip"], "any": [["no DB row", "no row", "inserts no rows", "unclaimable", "unsearchable", "invisible"]], "facts": "`--no-index` makes ingest insert no DB rows at all (ingest itself inserts none), so the moved files are unclaimable by workers, unsearchable and invisible to hash dedup. Use `--no-clip` instead: it registers rows and leaves CLIP to the fleet."}
{"id": "refile-link-not-rename", "area": "ingest", "prompt": "Why does refile-unknown-camera move files with os.link + unlink instead of os.rename or shutil.copy2?", "expect": ["EEXIST"], "any": [["overwrite", "clobber", "silently replace"]]}
{"id": "maintenance-cron-time", "area": "maintenance", "prompt": "Where does the nightly maintenance-sweep cron entry live on the NAS, and at what time does it run?", "any": [["18:30", "30 18", "6:30 ?pm"], ["crontab", "user", "cantimatt"]], "facts": "The nightly `maintenance-sweep --apply --no-colors` cron entry lives in the NAS user's crontab (flock-guarded) and runs daily at 18:30."}
{"id": "sweep-match-strict-only", "area": "maintenance", "prompt": "Does the nightly maintenance sweep run the temporal face matcher? How would you turn it on?", "any": [["strict", "\\bno\\b", "not", "opt-in"], ["--match-temporal", "match_temporal"]], "facts": "No: the sweep's match_faces stage is STRICT-ONLY. Temporal matching is opt-in: pass --match-temporal (match_temporal=True); never on by default."}
{"id": "exclusions-churn-fix", "area": "faces", "prompt": "The sweep's match_faces and resolve_dups stages used to apply the same ~6000 faces every night. What table stops that loop and what does it remember?", "expect": ["face_person_exclusions"], "any": [["pair", "pairing", "which person", "face.*person"]]}
{"id": "exclusion-strict-vs-temporal", "area": "faces", "prompt": "When a face's best match is a person barred by face_person_exclusions, how do the strict and the temporal matchers behave differently, and why must they not be unified?", "any": [["strict.*(next|fall|runner)", "fall.?through", "next best"], ["temporal.*(unmatched|leaves|refuses|no label)", "leaves.*unmatched", "min_gap", "ambigu"]], "facts": "Strict matcher: if the best match is barred by face_person_exclusions it falls through to the next in-tolerance person. Temporal matcher: it ranks the UNFILTERED field and leaves the face unmatched if the winner is barred. They must not be unified because filtering first would defeat TEMPORAL_MIN_GAP (the ambiguity gap) and promote ambiguous siblings."}
{"id": "rejected-match-source", "area": "faces", "prompt": "What does match_source='rejected' mean, why does it exist when dedupe_unmatched already did, and how do the matchers honour it?", "expect": ["rejected"], "any": [["MATCHABLE_SQL", "re-appl", "matcher.*skip", "filtered out"], ["dedupe_unmatched"]], "facts": "match_source='rejected' is a human 'no' written when a human clears a label, so it survives auto-matching (NULL would read as never-matched and the next sweep would re-apply the match). dedupe_unmatched is different: it stays matchable because the loser of a duplicate may be a different person. Both matchers honour it via the shared faces.MATCHABLE_SQL predicate (they filter it out)."}
{"id": "rival-test", "area": "faces", "prompt": "What is the face-label rival test, and why can it find errors the margin test structurally cannot?", "expect": ["same photo"], "any": [["unlabel", "untagged", "not.*registered", "swap"]], "facts": "The rival test asks whether the right person's face is another face in the SAME PHOTO (typically an unlabelled face, or one not registered as that person, so a swap is possible). It can find errors the margin test structurally cannot because it looks at the other faces in the same photo rather than only comparing the labelled face against references."}
{"id": "leave-one-burst-out", "area": "faces", "prompt": "Why does verify-face-labels exclude the whole burst rather than just the face itself when measuring a face against its person's references?", "expect": ["burst"], "any": [["impostor", "vouch", "same mistake", "other frame", "1 ?s", "one second", "collapse"]], "facts": "A face's burst siblings (frames about one second apart) carry the same mislabel and would vouch for it (the impostor looks like its own reference). Excluding only the face itself leaves those near-identical frames, which collapses the distance; excluding the whole burst (BURST_WINDOW_SECONDS) measures it against independent references."}
{"id": "ps-poll-not-setinterval", "area": "frontend", "prompt": "How should a frontend page poll a status endpoint, and why is setInterval wrong here?", "expect": ["PS\\.poll"], "any": [["settle", "previous", "came back", "in-flight", "stack", "40 (request )?threads", "starv"]], "facts": "Use PS.poll(fn, ms). setInterval fires whether or not the previous request has returned, so on a starved NAS requests stack up and fill the server's request threads (the status page wedged the box it reported on). PS.poll re-arms only after fn's promise settles, pauses while the tab is hidden, and stop() aborts the in-flight call."}
{"id": "frontend-ref-check", "area": "frontend", "prompt": "The frontend has no linter. What catches a helper that is called from a page which never defines it?", "expect": ["check-frontend-refs"]}
{"id": "restart-helper-container", "area": "frontend", "prompt": "Why can't /api/admin/restart run `docker compose up` for photosearch from inside the photosearch container, and what does it do instead?", "expect": ["helper"], "any": [["PID namespace", "dies", "killed", "kill", "stranded", "Created"]]}
{"id": "batch-zero-unprocessed-lies", "area": "ingest", "prompt": "In batch_state, why does count_unprocessed_photos returning 0 not mean a step is completed? Name the two cases.", "any": [["attempts", "exhausted", "blocked"], ["waiting", "description IS NOT NULL", "gated", "describe"]], "facts": "count_unprocessed_photos is a claim predicate, not progress, so 0 does not mean completed. Case 1: every photo is exhausted (attempts at the cap), which reads `blocked`. Case 2: the step is gated/waiting on an earlier step (e.g. no description yet), which reads `waiting`. `completed` means done == total."}
{"id": "batch-queued-source", "area": "ingest", "prompt": "Where does a batch step's `queued` state come from, and what must it never be derived from?", "expect": ["ingest_batch_jobs"], "any": [["fleet-status", "run-workers\\.sh --status", "process list"]]}
{"id": "detect-stacks-empty-list", "area": "maintenance", "prompt": "What happens if detect_stacks or run_stacking is called with photo_ids=[] (an empty list), and why does that matter?", "any": [["whole library", "entire library", "library", "no scope", "unscoped"], ["wipe", "clear", "wiped", "reset", "lost"]], "facts": "photo_ids=[] is read as 'no scope', i.e. the WHOLE library, not as no photos. A stage that passed [] would re-detect over the entire library and wipe/clear existing stacks (this has happened)."}
{"id": "geocode-oom-per-row", "area": "maintenance", "prompt": "Why did the geocode maintenance stage OOM-kill the NAS container, and what two fixes made it fit in memory?", "any": [["per.?row", "dict", "745"], ["_MIN_POPULATION", "population"], ["stream", "StringIO", "f\\.read"]], "facts": "RGeocoder.load keeps one Python dict per row, so the row count is the memory bill, which OOM-killed the NAS container. Fixes: (1) filter by population with _MIN_POPULATION = 1 (drop population-0 hamlets), (2) stream the CSV via RGeocoder(stream=f) instead of io.StringIO(f.read()), which held the CSV twice."}
{"id": "vec0-orphans", "area": "database", "prompt": "Why do orphan rows accumulate in clip_embeddings and face_encodings when photos or faces are deleted, and what cleans them?", "expect": ["cleanup-orphans"], "any": [["vec0", "virtual table"], ["cascade", "foreign"]]}
{"id": "api-intent-headers", "area": "patterns", "prompt": "When a Claude session calls the photosearch HTTP API directly with curl, which headers must it send and why?", "expect": ["X-Photosearch-Source", "X-Photosearch-Intent"]}
{"id": "sharpness-error-vs-absent", "area": "workers", "prompt": "In the sharpness backfill, what is recorded when a file fails to decode, versus when the file is simply not present on this machine?", "any": [["stored", "sharpness_json", "version.*set", "never re-decode"], ["not_local", "nothing", "absent", "records nothing", "not written"]], "facts": "A decode error is STORED (sharpness NULL, error in sharpness_json, sharpness_version set) so the bad file is never re-decoded. A file simply absent on this machine records nothing (reported as not_local) and writes no row."}
{"id": "mirror-fields-needs-nas", "area": "replica", "prompt": "After a sync-mode re-run from the image view on the replica, why might the result show mirrored:0, and what fixes it?", "expect": ["mirror-fields"], "any": [["404", "older", "redeploy", "deploy the NAS", "NAS.*M28"]], "facts": "The replica's mirror step calls NAS GET /api/photos/{id}/mirror-fields; a NAS image older than M28 returns 404, so the result shows mirrored:0. Fix: redeploy (deploy) the NAS first, then re-run."}
